namespace trion:
    use formal

    primitive behavior, moment

    section "The short sentence is the unit of meaning":
        A short sentence means a statement that has exactly one reading.
        The reading of a short sentence is exactly one contract.
        A specification is a set of short sentences.
        The formal meaning of a specification is the conjunction of the contracts of the short sentences of the specification.

        [SENTENCE-ONE-READING]
        The trion processor shall read each short sentence as exactly one contract.

        [SENTENCE-ONLY-UNIT]
        Each statement that the trion processor verifies shall be a short sentence.

    section "Satisfiable is less than consistent":
        A behavior satisfies a contract exactly when the assumption of the contract does not hold in the behavior or the guarantee of the contract holds in the behavior.
        A specification is satisfiable exactly when at least one behavior satisfies each contract of the specification.

        A normative sentence means a short sentence that obliges, forbids, recommends, or permits.
        A descriptive sentence means a short sentence that has no normative force.
        A possible situation means a situation that satisfies each descriptive sentence of a specification.
        A specification is consistent exactly when each possible situation satisfies the guarantee of each contract whose assumption holds in the situation.

        [SENTENCE-VACUOUS]
        The trion processor shall accept a specification as consistent only if each possible situation satisfies the guarantee of each contract whose assumption holds in the situation.

    section "What a contract is made of":
        A contract atom means an atom of the assumption or of the guarantee of a contract.
        The kind of a contract atom is act, forbid, discouraged, event, value, domain, or configuration.

        An act atom means a contract atom that states the completion of an action by a subject.
        A forbid atom means a contract atom that forbids an action.
        A discouraged atom means a contract atom that discourages an action.
        An event atom means a contract atom that states the occurrence of an event.
        A value atom means a contract atom that gives a value to an attribute.
        A domain atom means a contract atom that admits a value of an attribute.
        A configuration atom means a contract atom that states a feature of a system.

    section "A normative sentence and its contract":
        The modal verb of a normative sentence is "shall", "must", "should", or "may".
        The normative force of a normative sentence is obligation, prohibition, recommendation, or permission.
        An obligation means a normative sentence that requires an action.
        A prohibition means a normative sentence that forbids an action.
        A recommendation means a normative sentence that advises an action.
        A permission means a normative sentence that allows an action.

        The guarantee of an obligation holds an act atom, the negation of a forbid atom, and the negation of a discouraged atom.

        example OBLIGATION:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "The API shall stop."
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: (Act(API|stop) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|stop) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|stop))"

        The guarantee of a prohibition holds the negation of an act atom and a forbid atom.

        example PROHIBITION:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "The API shall not stop."
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: (¬Act(API|stop) ∧ Forbid(API|stop))"

        The guarantee of a recommendation holds the negation of a forbid atom and the negation of a discouraged atom.

        example RECOMMENDATION:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "The API should stop."
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: (¬Forbid(API|stop) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|stop))"

        The guarantee of a permission holds the negation of a forbid atom.

        example PERMISSION:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "The API may stop."
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: ¬Forbid(API|stop)"

        No behavior satisfies an obligation and a prohibition of the same action.

        example OBLIGATION-AGAINST-PROHIBITION:
            kind consistency
            input """
[A] The API shall stop.
[B] The API shall not stop.
"""
            expect inconsistent

    section "A condition is the assumption":
        A condition means a clause that restricts the situations of a short sentence.
        Each phrase of a short sentence belongs to the assumption or to the guarantee of the contract of the short sentence.
        Each condition of a short sentence belongs to the assumption of the contract of the short sentence.

        A "When" clause names an event, and the assumption holds at each occurrence of the event.

        example WHEN:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "When the door opens, the bell shall ring."
            expect "assume: Event(door open)"
            expect "guarantee: (Act(bell|ring) ∧ ¬Forbid(bell|ring) ∧ ¬Discouraged(bell|ring))"

        A "While" clause names a state, and the assumption holds throughout the state.

        example WHILE:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "While the door is open, the lamp shall be lit."
            expect "assume: Val(door, open)"
            expect "guarantee: Val(lamp, lit)"

        An "If" clause names an unwanted event.

        example IF:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "If the sensor fails, then the alarm shall sound."
            expect "assume: Event(sensor fail)"
            expect "guarantee: (Act(alarm|sound) ∧ ¬Forbid(alarm|sound) ∧ ¬Discouraged(alarm|sound))"

        A "Where" clause names a feature that a system includes.

        example WHERE:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "Where the car has a sunroof, the car shall have a sunroof control."
            expect "assume: Config(car have sunroof)"
            expect "guarantee: (Act(car|have sunroof control) ∧ ¬Forbid(car|have sunroof control) ∧ ¬Discouraged(car|have sunroof control))"

        A behavior satisfies each contract whose assumption does not hold in the behavior.

        example CONDITIONS-APART:
            kind consistency
            input """
[A] While the door is open, the lamp shall be lit.
[B] While the door is closed, the lamp shall not be lit.
"""
            expect consistent

    section "A descriptive sentence and its contract":
        A descriptive sentence has no modal verb.
        The contract of a descriptive sentence holds in each possible situation.
        A definition means a descriptive sentence that gives a meaning to a term.
        An enumeration means a descriptive sentence that lists the values of an attribute.

        example ENUMERATION:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "The door is open or closed."
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: (Dom(door, open) ∧ Dom(door, closed) ∧ (Val(door, open) ∨ Val(door, closed)))"

        example DEFINITION:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "A guest means a user without an account."
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: (Dom(guest, user) ∧ (¬Act(guest|has account) ∧ ¬Act(guest|have account)))"

        An enumeration closes the values of an attribute.

        example VALUE-OUTSIDE:
            kind consistency
            input """
[D] The door is open or closed.
[A] The door shall be ajar.
"""
            expect inconsistent

        An enumeration assigns a listed value to the attribute.

        example VALUE-MISSING:
            kind consistency
            input """
[D] The door is open or closed.
[A] While the alarm is active, the door shall not be open.
[B] While the alarm is active, the door shall not be closed.
"""
            expect inconsistent

        example VALUE-LEFT:
            kind consistency
            input """
[D] The door is open or closed.
[A] While the alarm is active, the door shall not be open.
"""
            expect consistent
